think Read KYRI + RADIANT_STYLE to be Kyri, then REMEMBER, the session-log README top rows, and REDS. Standfast: every living REDS row is closed or released; only #75 rests as custody-gated errata (a deep-debride, custody gate, not agent-closable). Clear to build.
think The Microkernel double-seat leads Lindy-first, yet its only ungated moves sit behind web-fetch/license gates (surfaced, never crossed), so this lap grows the live green edge: Season G open-media.
think The branch log named the crux-first siblings -- a disk-element builder, an 8-connected-component labeler, a Zhang-Suen thinning pass -- noting the crossing number branch owns IS that algorithm's own connectivity test. Chose Zhang-Suen: Lindy (a 1984 classic still taught for OCR/fingerprint/shape skeletonization), crux (a genuine parallel iterate-to-convergence algorithm with subtle simple-point conditions), and it stands on proven ground.
think Compose not braid: Zhang-Suen's A(P1) connectivity number is exactly branch's crossing number (same ring order N,NE,E,SE,S,SW,W,NW, same rising-edge arc count, same off-image-is-ground rule). Refactored image/branch.rye to expose ring_neighbors (the eight-neighbor gather) and crossing_from (the arc count) as the one source of truth, plus is_on as the one binary door; branch's own crossing_number now composes from them. branch re-GREEN, unchanged behavior.
think Built image/zhang_suen.rye: skeletonize deletes every removable boundary point in two alternating sub-iterations, all deletions decided from the same input plane and applied together (parallel), until a whole cycle removes nothing. A pixel is removable when B in 2..6 (this rung owns the neighbor count), A==1 (branch's crossing number, the simple-point test), and the sub-iteration's directional pair leaves a corner open (sub 1 SE, sub 2 NW). Termination a bound: each productive cycle removes >=1 pixel, at most the initial foreground count of cycles, the loop asserting the ceiling.
obs The REDUCTION crux corrected the hand-trace on metal (measurement beats memory): I first predicted a 3x3 block reduces to a plus, but the metal caught BlockSkeletonMissingPlus. Re-derived fully -- sub 1 removes six pixels (the four corners AND the two edge midpoints where condition d opens), leaving an L; sub 2 removes two of the three; the true result is the SINGLE CENTER PIXEL, the medial point of a square, in exactly one productive cycle (nine to one). Cleaner and exactly hand-verifiable; corrected the crux, the GREEN line, README, and the witness prose to the real result.
obs The LINE-SURVIVAL crux is the decisive anti-erosion proof: a one-pixel horizontal line is a fixed point (zero productive cycles) where a plain erosion would eat it from both ends -- its interior pixels have crossing number two (A != 1), its ends one neighbor (B < 2), so no pixel is ever removable. Plus a UNIT-WIDTH witness (no solid 2x2 square survives), anti-extensive, idempotence, opacity from a translucent source, QOI round-trip byte-for-byte, empty-source refusal by name, determinism.
obs Silo (council rota item 22, deep-read this lap): Zhang-Suen is a published algorithm; applied Silo -- understood the pattern, re-expressed it in Grain's own vocabulary (removable boundary point, the crossing number composed from branch), reproduced no external code, named the algorithm plainly where naming is clarity. Clean room held.
